  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{k},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{k}(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2(x-1)^{2}+4(x-1)^{3}-\u22ef+{{(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k+1}}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{the\u00a0}k\\text{th term}}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-4(x-1)+12(x-1)^{2}-\u22ef+{{(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{the\u00a0}k\\text{th term}}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)={\\sum }_{k=1}^{\\infty }(k+1)(-2)^{k}(x-1)^{k}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=k+1.\n$$",
